  "worked_solution": "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14131516/assets/ff1f735266f66879834e02ca7601e90e40dfa7929e5796d4a3f8cb4ff2828de7.png)\n\n  \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nNotice that, in this case, the set $A$ contains none of its boundary points. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. As we've seen, $A$ contains none of its boundary points.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A$ contains none of its boundary points, we must have $\\text{int}\\,A=A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
